virglrenderer: constant buffer 0 from a guest buffer

What: a stage's constant buffer 0 bound as a guest buffer (SET_UNIFORM_BUFFER
index 0) is now its plain constants (CONST[0..n]), as gallium means it. vrend
took those only from SET_CONSTANT_BUFFER. When the guest's last word for
the stage's slot 0 was a buffer, each draw reads the buffer's bytes back
(glGetBufferSubData: the guest writes new ones into the same buffer) and
uses them as if SET_CONSTANT_BUFFER had sent them, as many as the shader
declares; past the bound range they read as zero. A later
SET_CONSTANT_BUFFER wins again. Where the constants are a uniform block
bound per draw, the GPU copies them into the stage's own buffer
(glCopyBufferSubData: a read back makes the render thread wait for the GPU
on every converted frame); uniforms and a vertex shader's window read them
back. Desktop GL only (GLES
has no glGetBufferSubData: unchanged there). Applies on top of
virgl-const-uniform-buffer.patch (both constant paths).
Why: Mesa's st/mesa sends plain constants as data, but Mesa's own gallium
helpers bind a buffer: the VA-API video compositor (vl_compositor) binds its
colour matrix that way. Its shader read zeros, so every frame VA-API
converted to RGB was black (Google Chrome converts each decoded frame to
ARGB with it; FFmpeg's scale_vaapi gave B=G=R=0, A=255).
Upstreamable; not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-const-ubo.c case 22, in all three constant modes
(fails without: stripes 0 and 1 read zeros, stripe 3 the older constants).
